const e={note:"Ten currents through the Chinese esoteric disciplines — and the first thing to say is that they are not ten. Internal alchemy, laboratory alchemy, liturgy, talismanic writing, ordination registers, thunder ritual, visionary meditation, astral prognostication, the Changes and the pursuit of transcendence are ten angles on one correlative cosmology, in which qì 氣 (ch'i), the yīnyáng 陰陽 polarity and the five phases wǔxíng 五行 supply a single grammar for the body, the crucible, the altar, the sky, the calendar and the ground beneath a grave — so that a physician, a diviner, a siting-master reading fēngshuǐ 風水 and an ordained priest are all reading the same weather off different instruments, and a term met in one discipline will be found doing structural work in the next. The Daoist Canon, Dàozàng 道藏 (Tao-tsang) — the Zhengtong canon printed in 1445 with its Wanli supplement of 1607, some fifteen hundred titles — preserves them together and unsorted by our categories, which is itself the argument. Every Chinese string on this page is in traditional characters and has been checked sign by sign against Unicode; romanisation is Hanyu Pinyin with tone marks, with Wade-Giles given in parentheses on the first mention of a term a reader will meet in older scholarship, and the simplified form noted only where the difference changes the sense. Two warnings govern everything that follows: much of this material was transmitted with the operative part kept deliberately off the page — the jué 訣, the oral formula reserved for a master to give in person — so that texts which look like complete instructions are husks, and a page of unreadable graphs is unreadable by design rather than by damage; and Daoism has ordained clergy and practising congregations today, so what follows is history and description, with no operative instruction given for anything, and least of all for the alchemical preparations, which killed the people who trusted them.",themes:[{slug:"internal-alchemy",title:"Internal Alchemy",native:"內丹",translit:"nèidān",summary:"The body is the crucible. The vocabulary of lead, mercury, furnace and firing-time that the laboratory used for minerals is turned inward and made to describe what an adept does with his own vitality — a transposition that scholarship still argues was a translation, a borrowing, or a polemical claim.",paragraphs:["The system rests on a triad the tradition calls the three treasures, sān bǎo 三寶 — a phrase it shares, knowingly and a little awkwardly, with the Buddhist triratna, and which here names not Buddha, Dharma and Saṅgha but jīng 精, qì 氣 (ch'i) and shén 神. Jing is the dense, generative, seminal end of a person's endowment; qi is breath and vapour, the animating pneuma that moves; shen is spirit, the luminous knowing at the top of the scale. They are not three substances but one substance at three degrees of refinement, and the whole discipline is a graded distillation from the coarse toward the subtle, set out in a formula that every neidan (nei-tan) text repeats: liàn jīng huà qì 煉精化氣, refine the essence and transmute it into breath; liàn qì huà shén 煉氣化神, refine the breath and transmute it into spirit; liàn shén huán xū 煉神還虛, refine the spirit and revert to emptiness. Several lineages append a fourth, liàn xū hé dào 煉虛合道, refine emptiness and merge with the Dao (Tao); whether that fourth is a stage at all or the collapse of the entire ladder is a live doctrinal dispute and not a scholastic ornament. The compound nèidān itself is hard to date. The passage most often cited as its earliest occurrence stands in a vow-text, the Lìshìyuàn wén 立誓願文, attributed to the sixth-century monk Huìsī 慧思 (515–577), whose authenticity and dating have both been questioned; secure uses of the word as the name of a system belong to the Tang and after.","The work is located. The dāntián 丹田, the 'cinnabar field', is a site in the body — three of them in the developed schemes: a lower field below the navel, a middle field at the heart, an upper field in the head at the point called níwán 泥丸, the 'muddy pellet'. The term is older than internal alchemy proper. It is already at work in the Huángtíng jīng 黃庭經 and in Han meditation literature, where the fields are dwellings for the body's resident gods rather than vessels for a refining operation, so that neidan inherits a house already furnished. The circulation between them — up the spine along the dūmài 督脈, the governing vessel, over the crown and down the front along the rènmài 任脈, the conception vessel — is what English-language writing calls the microcosmic orbit, after xiǎo zhōutiān 小周天, the 'lesser celestial circuit'. A caution is owed here. The name is astronomical, a full revolution of the heavens, and as a settled label for this particular circuit it belongs to the later tradition and above all to Ming, Qing and modern practice manuals; Song neidan more often describes the same movement through firing-times and hexagram sequences, and a reader who carries the tidy modern diagram back into an eleventh-century text will find that it does not quite fit.","What the refining produces is described as a gestation. The shèngtāi 聖胎, the holy embryo, or simply the yīng'ér 嬰兒, the infant, is conceived within the adept, carried, nourished — the texts speak of a suckling of three years — and at length issued through the crown of the head in the exit of the spirit, chūshén 出神: a second and deathless body that can travel while the first sits still. The imagery is insistently obstetric in a corpus written overwhelmingly by and for men, and the tradition of women's inner alchemy, nǚdān 女丹, which developed its own sequence from the Ming onward and takes the practitioner's actual physiology as its starting point, both exploits that and complicates it. Whether the embryo is a metaphor, a subtle body or a literal second person is not a question the sources agree on, and there is a modern temptation to settle it on their behalf that is worth resisting: some texts are unmistakably figurative, others are not, and the same readers read both.","The lineages are tangled, and were tidied afterwards. The ZhōngLǚ 鍾呂 tradition takes its name from Zhōnglí Quán 鍾離權 and Lǚ Dòngbīn 呂洞賓 (Lü Tung-pin), a pair of transcendents whose historicity is at best thin — Lü is conventionally placed in the Tang and is today among the most widely worshipped figures in Chinese religion — and its principal texts, the Zhōng-Lǚ chuándào jí 鍾呂傳道集, attributed to Shī Jiānwú 施肩吾, and the Língbǎo bìfǎ 靈寶畢法, attributed to Zhongli Quan himself, are pseudonymous works of roughly the tenth century cast as dialogues between master and disciple. Zhāng Bóduān 張伯端 (traditionally 987–1082) wrote the Wùzhēn piān 悟真篇, 'Folios on Awakening to Reality', whose preface is dated 1075 and whose postface is dated 1078; it is the most influential neidan text there is, and is composed in regulated verse, quatrains and lyric ci of such compression that it has never been read without a commentary. He founded nothing. The Southern Lineage, nánzōng 南宗, that claims him was assembled retrospectively as a chain of five patriarchs running through Shí Tài 石泰, Xuē Dàoguāng 薛道光 and Chén Nán 陳楠 to Bái Yùchán 白玉蟾 (b. 1194), the first of them to have organised anything. The Northern Lineage is Quánzhēn 全真 (Ch'üan-chen), 'Complete Perfection', founded by Wáng Chóngyáng 王重陽 (1113–1170), who reported meeting Zhongli Quan and Lü Dongbin at Ganhe in 1159 — a hagiographical episode, and offered here as one — and who gathered in Shandong from 1167 the seven disciples the tradition calls the Seven Perfected: among them Sūn Bù'èr 孫不二, the one woman, and Qiū Chǔjī 丘處機 (1148–1227), whose journey west to Chinggis Khan, undertaken from 1220 and reaching him in 1222, won the order an imperial patent and made it, for a time, the dominant religious institution of north China. The textbook contrast — that the North puts inner nature before life-destiny, xìng 性 before mìng 命, and the South the reverse — is a formulation genuinely found in the sources, but it is a slogan applied after the fact to two bodies of practice that overlap a great deal.","That leaves the argument a reader should carry away. Neidan's technical vocabulary is, item for item, the vocabulary of the furnace: lead and mercury, qiān 鉛 and gǒng 汞; the tripod and the stove, dǐng 鼎 and lú 爐; the firing schedule, huǒhòu 火候; the reverted elixir, huándān 還丹. The oldest reading of this, argued by Joseph Needham and refined by Nathan Sivin, is straightforwardly developmental: laboratory alchemy failed, killed its patrons, and was interiorised, its terms retained as metaphors for a physiological practice. Isabelle Robinet pressed a sharper case — that neidan is not interiorised waidan at all but a distinct synthesis, drawing as heavily on Shangqing visionary meditation and Han Yijing cosmology as on the crucible, and taking over the alchemical lexicon as an authorising idiom, a claim to be the true reading of what the laboratory had been doing all along. On that account the arrow reverses. Neidan does not merely inherit waidan's language; it systematically re-reads waidan's language as having been about the body from the beginning, so that the Cāntóng qì 參同契 becomes, retroactively, an inner-alchemical scripture and its mineral sense the misunderstanding. Both readings survive in the current literature, and the honest position is that the transposition was neither purely a metaphor nor purely a discovery but a polemical act of interpretation, performed by people who were convinced they were recovering a meaning rather than inventing one."],terms:[{native:"內丹",translit:"nèidān",gloss:"Internal alchemy; simplified 内丹. The refining of jing, qi and shen within the body, using the laboratory's vocabulary"},{native:"精",translit:"jīng",gloss:"Essence; the dense generative substrate of a person, first of the three treasures and first term of the refining sequence"},{native:"氣",translit:"qì",gloss:"Breath, vapour, pneuma; simplified 气. The animating medium common to body, weather, landscape and rite — Wade-Giles ch'i"},{native:"神",translit:"shén",gloss:"Spirit; both the luminous knowing at the top of the refining scale and the ordinary word for a god"},{native:"丹田",translit:"dāntián",gloss:"'Cinnabar field'; the three loci of the work, lower, middle and upper — a term older than neidan itself"},{native:"煉精化氣",translit:"liàn jīng huà qì",gloss:"'Refine the essence and transmute it into breath'; the first stage of the standard three-stage sequence"},{native:"聖胎",translit:"shèngtāi",gloss:"Holy embryo; the immortal body gestated within the adept and issued through the crown of the head"},{native:"性命",translit:"xìngmìng",gloss:"Inner nature and life-destiny; the pair whose order of priority is said to distinguish the Northern and Southern lineages"}],texts:[{title:"Zhang Boduan, Wùzhēn piān 悟真篇",note:"'Folios on Awakening to Reality', preface dated 1075, postface 1078; the central Song neidan text, and unreadable without commentary. Translated with the commentarial tradition by Fabrizio Pregadio as Awakening to Reality (Golden Elixir Press, 2009)."},{title:"Zhōng-Lǚ chuándào jí 鍾呂傳道集",note:"The ZhongLü anthology, attributed to Shi Jianwu and cast as a dialogue between Zhongli Quan and Lü Dongbin; a pseudonymous work of roughly the tenth century, and the fountainhead of the later tradition."},{title:"Zhōuyì cāntóng qì 周易參同契",note:"The text both alchemies claim as ancestor, attributed to Wei Boyang; the received version is composite. Study and translation by Fabrizio Pregadio, The Seal of the Unity of the Three (Golden Elixir Press, 2011)."},{title:"Wang Chongyang, Chóngyáng lìjiào shíwǔ lùn 重陽立教十五論",note:"Fifteen Discourses on Establishing the Teaching — the short foundational statement of Quanzhen discipline, monastic in emphasis and cool about marvels."},{title:"Isabelle Robinet, Introduction à l'alchimie intérieure taoïste",note:"Cerf, 1995. The most searching account of neidan's structure, and the source of the argument that it is a synthesis rather than an interiorisation."},{title:"Fabrizio Pregadio and Lowell Skar, 'Inner Alchemy (Neidan)'",note:"In Livia Kohn, ed., Daoism Handbook (Brill, 2000) — the standard English survey of the field, with the textual history laid out."}]},{slug:"external-alchemy",title:"External Alchemy",native:"外丹",translit:"wàidān",summary:"The laboratory tradition took as its emblem a substance that could be driven out of itself and brought back — and pursued that reversal with mercury, lead and arsenic, in a corpus whose best-documented effect was to kill the people who trusted it.",paragraphs:["Everything begins with one mineral. Cinnabar, dānshā 丹砂 or zhūshā 硃砂, is mercuric sulphide, and it is red; heated, it gives up a silver liquid metal, shuǐyín 水銀, 'water silver', which is mercury; and that metal, recombined with sulphur, returns to a red sulphide. A substance leaves itself and comes back. No other reaction available to a medieval Chinese laboratory made so exact a picture of what the tradition wanted, and the name given to the goal says so plainly: huándān 還丹, the reverted cinnabar, the elixir that returns. The character dān 丹 accordingly does double duty throughout Chinese esoteric writing — cinnabar, and elixir, and by extension the art itself, so that waidan (wai-tan) and neidan are the outer and inner varieties of one word. This magazine names that reaction because it is the image on which a thousand years of thought was built. It gives no vessel, no proportion, no temperature and no sequence, and there is no version of these pages from which a preparation could be reconstructed.","The classic apologia is the Bàopǔzǐ 抱朴子 (Pao-p'u-tzu), 'The Master Who Embraces Simplicity', by Gě Hóng 葛洪 (born 283; his death is placed in 343 by some sources and around 363 by others), whose inner chapters, nèipiān 內篇, he says in his own postface he finished in about 317 and afterwards revised, so that 'about 320' is a conventional shorthand rather than a documented date. It is not a laboratory report but an argument. Ge Hong writes to convince sceptical literati that transcendence is real, that it is reached by technique, and that of all techniques the golden elixir, jīndān 金丹, stands first — herbs prolong life, he says in effect; only the elixir ends death. He names his transmission, from Zuǒ Cí 左慈 to his own great-uncle Gě Xuán 葛玄 to his master Zhèng Yǐn 鄭隱 and so to himself — a lineage claim, and to be read as one — and he names his scriptures, chief among them the Huángdì jiǔdǐng shéndān jīng 黃帝九鼎神丹經, the 'Scripture of the Divine Elixirs of the Nine Tripods of the Yellow Emperor', central text of the Tàiqīng 太清 or Great Clarity corpus and the oldest identifiable body of Chinese alchemical writing. He also tells us, repeatedly and without embarrassment, that he is withholding things: the scriptures are useless without the oral instruction, the decisive measures are in the jué, and a man who works from the book alone will fail. That is the ordinary condition of this literature and not an evasion peculiar to him.","The operation was conducted in a luted and sealed vessel — the tripod dǐng 鼎 set in its furnace lú 爐, closed with a mud whose name, liùyī ní 六一泥, the 'six-one mud', every treatise gives and whose composition this page does not — and the ingredients were, in the plainest terms, poisons. Mercury and its compounds, lead, and the arsenic minerals, realgar xiónghuáng 雄黃 chief among them, recur across the corpus. The consequence is not a modern inference but documented Chinese history. The Qing scholar Zhào Yì 趙翼 (1727–1814) gathered the cases in his Niàn'èr shǐ zhájì 廿二史劄記 under a heading observing how many Tang emperors ingested elixirs, and the sovereigns most often named — Xiànzōng 憲宗 (d. 820), Mùzōng 穆宗 (d. 824), Wǔzōng 武宗 (d. 846) and Xuānzōng 宣宗 (d. 859), with Tàizōng 太宗 (d. 649) standing at the head of the sequence — died in circumstances the histories associate with elixir-taking, though individual cases are still argued over by historians. Below the throne the same thing happened to officials, and one of the sharpest documents in the whole field is an epitaph by Hán Yù 韓愈 (768–824) for a friend and Erudite of the Imperial Academy, of the Lǐ 李 family, whose given name the editions read variously; in it Han Yu sets down, in a tone of controlled fury, six or seven men he had himself known who died the same death — among them Guī Dēng 歸登, who coughed blood for a decade before it took him, and Lǐ Xūzhōng 李虛中, whose name recurs below under Chinese Astrology as the traditional ancestor of the Four Pillars. Part of the tradition absorbed the disaster rather than conceding it, reclassifying elixir death as shījiě 尸解, release by a simulated corpse, so that the body left behind became evidence not of failure but of departure — a manoeuvre described under Immortality Practices, and to be read as what it is: a religious community making sense of its dead.","Laboratory alchemy did not so much end as get absorbed. It thins after the Tang, and the reasons offered — the scandal of the deaths, the rise of internal alchemy, the sheer expense — are each plausible and none sufficient alone; the corpus went on being copied and read long after it stopped being widely practised. Its residue passed into pharmacology, where mineral preparations remained part of the materia medica and the běncǎo 本草 literature inherited both the substances and a considerable body of hard-won knowledge about what they did to people. Joseph Needham built a long argument on this, that Chinese alchemy's real contribution was to a chemically-minded medicine rather than to any elixir. Nathan Sivin and Fabrizio Pregadio have since insisted, against the older framing, on reading the Taiqing texts as ritual and cosmological documents in their own right rather than as failed chemistry — the scriptures themselves treat the compounding as a rite, hedged about with purifications, taboos, retreats, offerings and the choice of an auspicious mountain, in which the change in the substance and the change in the operator are not two events. Both things are true at once, and the field is the more interesting for holding them together."],terms:[{native:"外丹",translit:"wàidān",gloss:"External or laboratory alchemy; the compounding of elixirs from minerals, as against the interior work of neidan"},{native:"丹砂",translit:"dānshā",gloss:"Cinnabar, mercuric sulphide; also written 硃砂 zhūshā. The red mineral at the centre of the tradition"},{native:"水銀",translit:"shuǐyín",gloss:"'Water silver' — mercury, driven from cinnabar by heat and returnable to a red sulphide"},{native:"還丹",translit:"huándān",gloss:"The 'reverted elixir'; the substance that departs from itself and comes back, and the emblem of the whole art"},{native:"金丹",translit:"jīndān",gloss:"The golden elixir; Ge Hong's supreme technique, ranked above all herbal and gymnastic methods"},{native:"鼎",translit:"dǐng",gloss:"The tripod or crucible, set within the furnace lú 爐; later reused by internal alchemy for regions of the body"},{native:"太清",translit:"Tàiqīng",gloss:"Great Clarity; the earliest identifiable corpus of Chinese alchemical scripture, and a heaven of that name"},{native:"雄黃",translit:"xiónghuáng",gloss:"Realgar, an arsenic sulphide; named here because the toxicity of the corpus is a historical fact, not because any use is described"}],texts:[{title:"Ge Hong, Bàopǔzǐ nèipiān 抱朴子內篇",note:"The inner chapters, finished by Ge Hong's own account in about 317 and revised afterwards: the defence of transcendence as technique, and the earliest sustained account of the alchemical enterprise. Translated by James R. Ware as Alchemy, Medicine, Religion in the China of A.D. 320 (MIT Press, 1966)."},{title:"Huángdì jiǔdǐng shéndān jīng jué 黃帝九鼎神丹經訣",note:"The Nine Tripods scripture with its Tang commentary — the core Taiqing text, and the one Ge Hong ranks first. Studied in detail by Pregadio."},{title:"Zhōuyì cāntóng qì 周易參同契",note:"Read by the later tradition as the ancestor of both alchemies; its hexagram machinery supplied the language of the firing schedule. See Yijing Cosmology."},{title:"Fabrizio Pregadio, Great Clarity: Daoism and Alchemy in Early Medieval China",note:"Stanford University Press, 2006. The standard study of the Taiqing corpus, and the argument for reading it as ritual rather than proto-chemistry."},{title:"Nathan Sivin, Chinese Alchemy: Preliminary Studies",note:"Harvard University Press, 1968. The book that put the field on a rigorous footing in English; it turns on a translation and study of the Dānjīng yàojué 丹經要訣 attributed to Sun Simiao."},{title:"Joseph Needham et al., Science and Civilisation in China, vol. V, parts 2–5",note:"Cambridge University Press, 1974–83. Vast, indispensable, and to be read now with the corrective that its chemical framing is not the sources' own."}]},{slug:"ritual-daoism",title:"Ritual Daoism",native:"齋醮",translit:"zhāijiào",summary:"A Daoist priest is an officeholder rather than a magician: what he performs is the drafting, sealing and delivery of documents to an administration — and the liturgical corpus that survives tells us what ought to have been done far more reliably than what was.",paragraphs:["The compound zhāijiào 齋醮 joins two words with separate histories. Zhāi 齋 is fasting, purification, retreat: in classical usage the abstinence preceding a sacrifice, and in medieval Daoism the name of a whole class of rites, often penitential, frequently for the dead, conducted over days by a company in seclusion. Jiào 醮 was originally a libation — the word appears in the classical ritual canon for the wine offered at a capping or a wedding — and became the name of an offering to the stars, then, by the Song, the name of the great communal rite of cosmic renewal that a village or a temple commissions and that may run three, five or seven days. Together they are the ordinary modern term for Daoist liturgy. The man who performs it is a dàoshì 道士 (tao-shih), a 'gentleman of the Dao', ordained, holding a rank and a register; in the Zhengyi tradition he is normally married and lives in a household rather than a monastery, which disconcerts visitors who arrived expecting monks.","The institutional line runs from Zhāng Dàolíng 張道陵, to whom Lord Lao is said to have appeared on Mount Heming, Hèmíng Shān 鶴鳴山, in Sichuan in 142 and conferred the Covenant of Orthodox Unity with the Powers, zhèngyī méngwēi 正一盟威. That date and that scene are traditional: no contemporary source records them, the earliest accounts are considerably later and in part hostile, and they are set down here as the tradition's own founding narrative rather than as established fact. What is historically firm is that by the later second century an organised movement existed in the west — called the Way of the Five Pecks of Rice, wǔdǒumǐ dào 五斗米道, by outsiders and the Way of the Celestial Masters, tiānshī dào 天師道, by itself — with parishes, registered households, appointed officers and an elaborate discipline; and that Zhang Daoling's grandson Zhāng Lǔ 張魯 governed a polity in Hanzhong until his surrender to Cao Cao in 215. Its distinctive instrument was the written petition. In the rite of submitting a memorial, shàngzhāng 上章, the priest composes a document — dated, addressed, formally styled, naming the petitioner and the complaint — announces it, and dispatches it by burning, whereupon spirit-messengers resident in his own body carry it upward. Illness was met with confession in writing: the three handwritten documents to the Officers of Heaven, Earth and Water, sānguān shǒushū 三官手書. The model is unmistakable and was never concealed. It is the Han civil service, projected onto the cosmos and then addressed in its own paperwork.","The rite is performed in a marked-out arena, the dàochǎng 道場, erected for the occasion inside a temple or a public space and dismantled afterwards: an altar, tán 壇, oriented and lamped, with scrolls of the celestial officers hung to make the four directions and the centre present, and a rite of sealing that fences the ground against intrusion. The repertoire that fills it is largely Lingbao. The Língbǎo 靈寶 (Ling-pao) scriptures were produced around 400 by Gě Cháofǔ 葛巢甫, of Ge Hong's family line, and they changed everything by importing wholesale from Buddhism — universal salvation, world-ages, the transfer of merit to ancestors — so that the Daoist rite acquired a congregation and a purpose beyond the individual adept. The Dùrén jīng 度人經, the 'Scripture for the Salvation of Humanity', is its emblem and is chanted still. The codification is the work of two men. Lù Xiūjìng 陸修靜 (406–477) catalogued the scriptures in 471 under the Three Caverns and gave the zhai its systematic form; Dù Guāngtíng 杜光庭 (850–933), at the end of the Tang, compiled the great manuals — among them the Tàishàng huánglù zhāiyí 太上黃籙齋儀, the protocol of the Yellow Register retreat for the dead — that carried a workable liturgy into the Song and so to the present.","The distinction between priest and spirit-medium is one the tradition itself draws sharply, and it is worth getting right. The daoshi is literate, ordained, and acts by virtue of an office recorded in a register; he does not lose consciousness, does not speak in a god's voice, and addresses the celestial administration as a colleague of lower rank submitting paperwork. The medium — tóngjī 童乩 in the Hokkien-speaking south, the tang-ki of the ethnographic literature, the same two graphs reversed in Mandarin usage as jītóng 乩童 — is chosen by a god rather than ordained, is possessed rather than commissioned, speaks as the deity in the first person, and in many local traditions mortifies his own body as proof of it. Kristofer Schipper, who was himself ordained in Tainan in 1964 and wrote from inside, made this contrast central to his account of the Daoist body. It should not be hardened into a caste divide, and neither office is being ranked here. At a village festival the two collaborate constantly — a medium may diagnose what is wrong and identify which god is owed what, and the priest then performs the jiao that regularises it — and the community sees no contradiction whatever.","Now the caution, which qualifies nearly everything above. The Canon preserves an enormous body of kēyí 科儀, ritual protocols: rubrics, sequences, texts to be intoned, movements to be made. These are prescriptive documents. They record what a compiler held should be done, often in idealised form, often at variance with one another, and frequently in order to assert one lineage's practice against another's. Records of actual medieval performance are thin — some stele inscriptions, a few court notices, occasional hostile mentions in secular sources, the Dunhuang manuscripts. Given the extraordinary richness of modern fieldwork in Taiwan, Fujian and among the diaspora, above all John Lagerwey's and Schipper's, there is a real methodological temptation to read the living rite backwards into the eleventh century, and it is a temptation precisely because the continuities are so striking. But nine hundred years is nine hundred years. The prudent reader treats the medieval manuals as a normative literature standing in an uncertain relation to practice, and treats the modern rite as evidence for the modern rite — which is a living tradition with its own authority, and not a fossil preserved for scholars to read."],terms:[{native:"齋醮",translit:"zhāijiào",gloss:"Daoist liturgy; the standard modern compound joining the retreat (zhai) and the offering (jiao)"},{native:"道士",translit:"dàoshì",gloss:"'Gentleman of the Dao'; an ordained priest holding a register and a ritual rank — Wade-Giles tao-shih"},{native:"齋",translit:"zhāi",gloss:"Purification, fast, retreat; and the name of a class of medieval rites, often penitential or for the dead"},{native:"醮",translit:"jiào",gloss:"Offering; originally a libation, later an astral offering, and by the Song the great communal rite of renewal"},{native:"上章",translit:"shàngzhāng",gloss:"The submission of a memorial; the composition, announcement and dispatch by burning of a document to the celestial administration"},{native:"道場",translit:"dàochǎng",gloss:"The ritual arena; a sacred area erected for the rite, oriented, lamped, sealed against intrusion, and afterwards dismantled"},{native:"科儀",translit:"kēyí",gloss:"Ritual protocol; the prescriptive manuals that make up most of what survives of medieval liturgy"},{native:"童乩",translit:"tóngjī",gloss:"Spirit-medium; the tang-ki of southern practice, 乩童 jītóng in Mandarin order — possessed rather than ordained, and the priest's frequent collaborator"}],texts:[{title:"Dùrén jīng 度人經",note:"The Scripture for the Salvation of Humanity, the emblematic Lingbao text of about 400, still in liturgical use. Translated in Stephen Bokenkamp, Early Daoist Scriptures (California, 1997)."},{title:"Lu Xiujing, Lù xiānshēng dàomén kēlüè 陸先生道門科略",note:"Abridged Codes for the Daoist Community — a fifth-century statement of what the church should look like, and a jeremiad about how far it had drifted."},{title:"Du Guangting, Tàishàng huánglù zhāiyí 太上黃籙齋儀",note:"The protocol of the Yellow Register retreat; the most substantial single monument of Tang liturgical compilation, and the bridge to Song practice."},{title:"John Lagerwey, Taoist Ritual in Chinese Society and History",note:"Macmillan, 1987. Based on fieldwork with the Tainan priest Chén Róngshèng 陳榮盛; the classic account of a jiao performed, set against the textual tradition."},{title:"Kristofer Schipper, The Taoist Body",note:"University of California Press, 1993 (French original Le corps taoïste, 1982). Written by an ordained participant; the source of the priest-and-medium distinction as usually stated."},{title:"Terry Kleeman, Celestial Masters: History and Ritual in Early Daoist Communities",note:"Harvard University Asia Center, 2016. The current standard account of the early movement, its parishes, its discipline and its documents."}]},{slug:"talismanic-writing",title:"Talismanic Writing",native:"符",translit:"fú",summary:"A fú is not a spell but a document — one half of a tally whose counterpart is held in heaven, drawn in a script said to have condensed out of primordial breath before any human hand wrote, and issued under seal by an officer entitled to issue it.",paragraphs:["Begin with the word, because the word carries the whole logic. Fú 符 means a tally: an object of bamboo, wood, bronze or jade, inscribed and then broken in two, one half retained by the authority and one issued to the agent, so that the agent's half proves his commission by fitting the other. Warring States and Han armies used bronze tiger tallies, hǔfú 虎符, cast in halves with a text running across the break, and no general moved troops without producing his. Daoism took the object and the logic together. A talisman is one half of a tally whose counterpart is held in the celestial administration; the strange graphs upon it are the broken edge, and their efficacy consists in matching. This is why the tradition speaks of talismans being verified, and why a forged or carelessly copied one simply fails, in the manner of a document with the wrong seal failing at a yamen gate. It also explains why fú and lù 籙, talisman and register, are so constantly paired: the register is the commission, and the talisman is the instrument that the commission entitles one to use.","On the doctrine the tradition supplies, these graphs are not human writing at all. Fúwén 符文, talismanic writing, is held to have taken shape spontaneously, zìrán 自然, out of the primordial breaths at the beginning of a world-age: coagulations of qi that condensed into configurations in the void, hung there in the heavens, and were only afterwards copied down by the Perfected and transmitted to men. The Lingbao corpus gives this its fullest form in the Five Tablets in Perfected Writing, wǔpiān zhēnwén 五篇真文, graphs said to have precipitated out of the beginning and to sustain the five directions; the general term for such originary graphs is zhēnwén 真文, perfected writ, or tiānshū 天書, celestial writing, and the flowing manner in which they are drawn is called yúnzhuàn 雲篆, 'cloud seal script'. Two consequences follow that are easy to miss. First, the talisman on the page is a copy of an original that exists independently of it, which is why fidelity in copying matters so intensely and why the sources fret continually about corrupt transmission. Second, the script is prior to writing rather than a variety of it: the claim is not that these are secret letters but that ordinary letters are a degenerate descendant of these — an inversion that gives Daoist graphology a cosmological rather than a cryptographic footing.","In practice the graphs look like what they claim. They are elongated and calligraphic, built from recognisable components in unrecognisable combinations. Seal-script elements are common; so are the characters chì 敕, 'by command', and lìng 令, 'order', which frame a talisman exactly as an edict is framed; so are constellation diagrams, dots joined by lines, the Dipper above all; so are composite graphs, hétǐzì 合體字, in which a deity's several-character title is compressed into one dense sign. The oldest surviving Chinese examples of the type are the fùwén 複文, 'compound graphs', of the Tàipíng jīng 太平經, built by stacking ordinary characters one upon another. The finished talisman is drawn, stamped with a wooden seal cut with the name of a celestial office, and then dispatched — which in this idiom means burnt, since burning is how a document is conveyed to the other world, exactly as petitions and spirit money are conveyed. Its ash may be stirred into water and drunk, fúshuǐ 符水, a practice attested from the earliest Celestial Master healing and met with still; the point is not pharmacology but delivery, the document being carried into the body it concerns.","The insistence that these are documents and not spells is not a modern scholarly nicety. It is what the material shows. A talisman carries a date, an addressee, an issuing authority and a seal. It works by jurisdiction: the spirits it commands are subordinates within an administration, and the officer's warrant to command them derives from his rank, so that the identical graph in the hand of an unordained person is simply paper. Anna Seidel traced the model back behind Daoism to the Han apocrypha and the imperial regalia, showing how heaven's mandate to a dynasty and heaven's commission to a priest are versions of one another; it is her argument, more than any other, that dislodged the older habit of reading these objects as sympathetic magic. One final point, and it is the reason so many pages of the Canon are opaque: the drawn graph is generally the least of it. The manner of drawing, the breath held or swallowed while drawing, the deity to be actualised, the incantation to be spoken, the moment in the sexagenary cycle at which the thing is valid — these are the kǒujué 口訣, the oral formulae, and they were not written down. A page of talismans without them is not a corrupt text. It is a complete text, of which the reader has been given the half that could safely be printed."],terms:[{native:"符",translit:"fú",gloss:"Talisman; and before that a tally split between two parties, whose halves prove a commission by matching"},{native:"符文",translit:"fúwén",gloss:"Talismanic writing; held to be the spontaneous coagulation of primordial qi, copied by the Perfected rather than composed"},{native:"雲篆",translit:"yúnzhuàn",gloss:"'Cloud seal script'; the flowing calligraphic manner in which celestial graphs are rendered"},{native:"真文",translit:"zhēnwén",gloss:"Perfected writ; the originary graphs, of which the Lingbao Five Tablets are the great instance"},{native:"天書",translit:"tiānshū",gloss:"Celestial writing; script prior to human script, of which ordinary characters are held to be the descendant"},{native:"符水",translit:"fúshuǐ",gloss:"Talisman water; the ash of a burnt talisman stirred into water and drunk — delivery of a document into the body"},{native:"虎符",translit:"hǔfú",gloss:"Tiger tally; the bronze military commission split in halves, the secular object from which the whole logic derives"},{native:"複文",translit:"fùwén",gloss:"'Compound graphs' of the Taiping jing, stacked from ordinary characters; simplified 复文, 复 absorbing both 複 and 復 (覆 remains a separate graph in current standard usage)"}],texts:[{title:"Yuánshǐ wǔlǎo chìshū yùpiān zhēnwén tiānshū jīng 元始五老赤書玉篇真文天書經",note:"The core Lingbao scripture of the Perfected Writs of the Five Ancients; the fullest statement of the doctrine that the graphs condensed out of primordial breath."},{title:"Tàipíng jīng 太平經",note:"The Scripture of Great Peace — an anonymous and much-layered text whose surviving chapters preserve the fùwén compound graphs, the earliest substantial body of Chinese talismanic writing."},{title:"Dàofǎ huìyuán 道法會元",note:"The great late compendium of Song and Yuan ritual, anonymous and composite, and the richest single reservoir of talismans, seals and their accompanying rubrics. See Thunder Rites."},{title:"Anna Seidel, 'Imperial Treasures and Taoist Sacraments'",note:"In Tantric and Taoist Studies in Honour of R. A. Stein, vol. 2 (1983). The essay that grounded talisman and register in the Han apocrypha and the imperial regalia."},{title:"Stephen Bokenkamp, Early Daoist Scriptures",note:"University of California Press, 1997. Annotated translations of the foundational Celestial Master and Lingbao texts, including the Perfected Writs material."},{title:"Kristofer Schipper and Franciscus Verellen, eds., The Taoist Canon: A Historical Companion to the Daozang",note:"University of Chicago Press, 2004, three volumes. The indispensable guide to what is in the Canon, title by title, with dates and attributions weighed."}]},{slug:"spirit-registers",title:"Spirit Registers",native:"籙",translit:"lù",summary:"The register is an ordination document and a roster in one. It names the celestial officers an initiate may summon, and by naming them makes him an officeholder — which is how a religion of mountain hermits became a bureaucracy of the invisible.",paragraphs:["Lù 籙 is a register, a roll, a list of names — an ordinary administrative word before it is a religious one. A Daoist register is a document conferred at ordination which enumerates the celestial officers placed at the recipient's disposal: generals and clerks, jiānglì 將吏, with their numbers, their titles, sometimes their appearance and sometimes their drawn images, together with the talismans by which they are summoned. To receive it, shòulù 受籙, is to be ordained. The register is simultaneously the diploma, the warrant of office and the operative equipment, and in this system there is no way to be a priest without one. It follows that the register defines the man: his title, his ritual rank, fǎwèi 法位, the formula with which he announces himself at the head of a petition, and the range of spirits he may lawfully command are all read off the document he holds. At his death it is returned or burnt.","Transmission was graded, and it began early. In the Celestial Master tradition children received registers young — the sources describe a register of one general, then of ten, then of seventy-five, each stage conferring a larger establishment — and the ordination manuals set the ages at which each was appropriate. The detail that best shows the system's character concerns marriage: sources of the tradition describe a husband's and a wife's registers of seventy-five generals being combined at their union into a single register of a hundred and fifty, the household thereby becoming one administrative unit with one staff. Adult grades continued upward through the register of the Meritorious Director, dūgōng 都功, toward the full Covenant registers. A Daoist career was thus legible as a sequence of documents in the way an official's career was legible as a sequence of appointments, and the parallel was intended.","Above the Zhengyi registers the medieval church built a ladder out of its own library. The Three Caverns, sāndòng 三洞 — Dòngzhēn 洞真 for the Shangqing scriptures, Dòngxuán 洞玄 for the Lingbao, Dòngshén 洞神 for the Sanhuang — were devised by Lu Xiujing in 471 as a scheme for cataloguing the canon, and promptly became something else: a curriculum. An ordinand advanced by receiving, in fixed order, the registers and scriptures of successive divisions, culminating in Shangqing, with the Four Supplements, sìfǔ 四輔, added in the following century and fitted in as ancillary grades. This is why the structure of the Daoist Canon is also the structure of a priestly life, and why the Canon's arrangement is unintelligible if read as a library catalogue alone. That the system was really operated and not merely described we know from an exceptional survival: Charles Benn reconstructed in detail the ordination of two Tang princesses, daughters of the emperor Ruizong, in 711 — the rite, its officiants, its cost and its politics. Three masters presided, an ordination master, a sponsor and a supervisor, which is still the pattern.","The relation between register and talisman is the relation between a commission and a tool, and the two circulate together as fúlù 符籙. Chinese scholarship has long used that compound to name one of two great divisions of Daoism, the 'talisman and register' schools, set against the dāndǐng 丹鼎 or 'elixir and tripod' schools of the alchemists. The typology is convenient and should be handled with tongs. It is a late heuristic, largely of the Ming and after, and no medieval Daoist described himself as belonging to either party; Bai Yuchan, to take the obvious counter-example, was a leading inner alchemist and a leading ritual master at once, and the thunder traditions treated the two as a single competence.","What the register finally did was constitutional. By making authority a matter of documented office rather than of personal charisma, healing power or ascetic achievement, it produced a religion in which the invisible world has an establishment — a chain of command, defined jurisdictions, and paperwork. Within that doctrine, as Schipper and Lagerwey have both set it out, the ordained priest holds a rank in the celestial administration which stands above that of most local temple deities, who are understood as functionaries of the same administration; this is a claim internal to Daoist ritual theology, not a verdict on the worth of the cults concerned. It is the ground on which the tradition has historically stood above popular cult while ministering to it, and the reason the same priest can be engaged by a village to discipline its own god. Nor is this antiquarian. Registers are still conferred: Zhengyi ordinations resumed at Longhu Shan 龍虎山 in 1991, at first for candidates from Taiwan and overseas and from 1995 for candidates on the mainland, after a long interruption there, while transmission was never broken in Taiwan or among the overseas communities — so that a priest today holds a document of the same kind as the one a fifth-century manual describes."],terms:[{native:"籙",translit:"lù",gloss:"Register; simplified 箓. The ordination document listing the celestial officers an initiate is entitled to summon"},{native:"受籙",translit:"shòulù",gloss:"To receive a register; the act that constitutes ordination and confers ritual standing"},{native:"符籙",translit:"fúlù",gloss:"Talisman and register together; also the name later scholarship gives to the liturgical, as against alchemical, side of Daoism"},{native:"將吏",translit:"jiānglì",gloss:"'Generals and clerks'; the celestial staff enumerated on a register, numbered and titled like an establishment table"},{native:"都功",translit:"dūgōng",gloss:"The Meritorious Director; a senior grade in the Zhengyi sequence, and the register that confers it"},{native:"三洞",translit:"sāndòng",gloss:"The Three Caverns; Lu Xiujing's 471 classification of the canon, which doubled as the curriculum of a priestly career"},{native:"度師",translit:"dùshī",gloss:"Ordination master; one of the three masters who preside at a transmission, with a sponsor and a supervisor"},{native:"法位",translit:"fǎwèi",gloss:"Ritual rank; the office a priest holds, announced at the head of every document he submits"}],texts:[{title:"Zhèngyī fǎwén tàishàng wàilù yí 正一法文太上外籙儀",note:"The Zhengyi protocol for the outer registers, anonymous and of uncertain date — the source for the childhood grades and for the combining of a couple's registers at marriage."},{title:"Lu Xiujing, Lù xiānshēng dàomén kēlüè 陸先生道門科略",note:"Fifth-century codes for the Daoist community, and the document behind the Three Caverns as an ordination ladder rather than a shelf-mark."},{title:"Tàishàng sānwǔ zhèngyī méngwēi lù 太上三五正一盟威籙",note:"The Covenant registers themselves, in the form the Canon preserves them: rosters of officers, with the talismans that summon them. Revealed rather than authored, on the tradition's own account."},{title:"Charles D. Benn, The Cavern-Mystery Transmission: A Taoist Ordination Rite of A.D. 711",note:"University of Hawaii Press, 1991. A documented ordination reconstructed in full — the best evidence anywhere that the ladder was climbed and not merely drawn."},{title:"Franciscus Verellen, Imperiled Destinies: The Daoist Quest for Deliverance in Medieval China",note:"Harvard University Asia Center, 2019. On petition, register and the accounting of fate in the Celestial Master tradition."},{title:"Terry Kleeman, Celestial Masters",note:"Harvard University Asia Center, 2016. The parish organisation, the household registers and the discipline within which the ordination grades make sense."}]},{slug:"thunder-rites",title:"Thunder Rites",native:"雷法",translit:"léifǎ",summary:"The one great liturgical invention of the Song: exorcistic and rain-making rites in which the officiant does not petition the thunder gods but generates the thunder within his own body first, and only then discharges it.",paragraphs:["Thunder rites are the Song's great ritual innovation, and their novelty was not concealed. From the late eleventh century a cluster of new traditions appears, each claiming a fresh revelation, each organised around a division of the celestial administration that had not previously held this prominence: the Thunder Department, léibù 雷部. Its head is the Celestial Worthy of the Sound of Thunder and Universal Transformation, Léishēng Pǔhuà Tiānzūn 雷聲普化天尊, usually given the fuller title Jiǔtiān Yìngyuán 九天應元, Responding Prime of the Nine Heavens. Beneath him serve marshals with surnames, biographies and iconography — Dèng 鄧, Xīn 辛, Zhāng 張, Táo 陶 — and beside him stand older figures of popular religion, the Duke of Thunder, Léigōng 雷公, and the Mother of Lightning, Diànmǔ 電母, who long predate the rites and were annexed by them. The rites themselves are generically the Five Thunders, wǔléi 五雷, thunder correlated with the five phases and the five directions, so that the department is a cosmological structure rather than a weather bureau.","The first of the new traditions to reach the throne was Shénxiāo 神霄, the Divine Empyrean. Its vehicle at court was Lín Língsù 林靈素 (d. c. 1119), who persuaded the emperor Huizong (r. 1100–1126) that he was the incarnate elder son of the Jade Emperor, obtained a state-sponsored network of Shenxiao temples, and for some years exercised an influence over religious policy that the dynastic historians, writing after the catastrophe of 1126, recorded with contempt. Lin was a courtier. The ritual substance of Shenxiao is owed rather to Wáng Wénqīng 王文卿 (1093–1153), who is credited with systematising the thunder methods and to whom much of the later corpus is attributed — an attribution, it should be said, that grew after his death. Hostile official sources and the tradition's own hagiography have to be read against each other here, and neither is disinterested.","The second tradition, Qīngwēi 清微, Pure Tenebrity, presents itself as a synthesis: Shangqing, Lingbao, Zhengyi and thunder ritual gathered into one transmission, traced to a woman, Zǔ Shū 祖舒, placed in the ninth or tenth century, and given scholastic form in the late Song by Huáng Shùnshēn 黃舜申 (b. 1224). The lineage account is retrospective and hagiographic in the ordinary way, and no independent record of Zu Shu survives. What matters is the doctrine Qingwei made explicit, which is the hinge of the entire subject. Thunder ritual is grounded in internal alchemy. The officiant refines his own jing, qi and shen, gathers the thunder within his own body — the texts locate it at the heart, or in the lower field, and describe it as an energy accumulated and compressed — and only then projects it outward through the hand-seals, shǒujué 手訣, the incantation, the talisman and the pacing of the stars. The formula the corpus repeats is that refined within, it becomes the elixir; deployed without, it becomes the rite: nèiliàn chéng dān 內煉成丹, wàiyòng chéng fǎ 外用成法. Bái Yùchán 白玉蟾, reckoned fifth patriarch of the Southern inner-alchemical lineage and a celebrated thunder master, is the figure in whom the two competences are most conspicuously one person, and his recorded sayings argue the identity at length.","What the rites were for was, in the main, two things. Rain: drought was a matter of state, magistrates and courts commissioned rain rituals, and a thunder master's reputation was made or broken on them — with the result that this is one of the few areas of Daoist practice for which a certain amount of unfriendly external documentation exists, since a failed rain rite is a public event. And exorcism: the kǎozhào 考召 rite, 'interrogation and summons', in which the demon causing an illness or an affliction is summoned, arrested, questioned under the authority of a celestial office, tried and sentenced — a procedure conducted in the forms of a magistrate's court, because that is what it is modelled upon, and in which the afflicted person may serve as the site of the interrogation. The corpus preserving all this is the Dàofǎ huìyuán 道法會元, the 'Corpus of Daoist Ritual', 268 chapters compiled anonymously at the end of the Yuan or the beginning of the Ming, overwhelmingly thunder material and the single richest source for Song and Yuan liturgy anywhere. These rites did not become historical: they remain in the repertoire of Zhengyi priests in Taiwan, Fujian and elsewhere, and the martial vocabulary of the ritual — the hand-seals, the paces, the sword — is among the most visible things a visitor to a modern jiao will see performed."],terms:[{native:"雷法",translit:"léifǎ",gloss:"Thunder rites; the Song ritual synthesis in which inner-alchemical refinement precedes and empowers external ritual action"},{native:"雷部",translit:"léibù",gloss:"The Thunder Department; the division of the celestial administration under which the marshals and the thunder gods serve"},{native:"五雷",translit:"wǔléi",gloss:"The Five Thunders; thunder correlated with the five phases and directions, and the generic name of the methods"},{native:"神霄",translit:"Shénxiāo",gloss:"Divine Empyrean; the tradition that reached Huizong's court through Lin Lingsu and supplied the thunder rites their first patronage"},{native:"清微",translit:"Qīngwēi",gloss:"Pure Tenebrity; the synthesising tradition that made explicit the grounding of thunder ritual in internal alchemy"},{native:"內煉",translit:"nèiliàn",gloss:"Inner refining; the alchemical work the officiant performs upon himself before any rite is deployed outward"},{native:"考召",translit:"kǎozhào",gloss:"'Interrogation and summons'; the exorcistic rite in which an afflicting demon is arrested, tried and sentenced"},{native:"祈雨",translit:"qíyǔ",gloss:"Praying for rain; the public and politically consequential use of thunder ritual, commissioned by magistrates and courts"}],texts:[{title:"Dàofǎ huìyuán 道法會元",note:"Corpus of Daoist Ritual, 268 chapters, anonymous, late Yuan or early Ming. Predominantly thunder material, and the indispensable source for Song and Yuan liturgy."},{title:"Gāoshàng shénxiāo yùqīng zhēnwáng zǐshū dàfǎ 高上神霄玉清真王紫書大法",note:"A major Shenxiao ritual compendium, revealed rather than authored on its own account, preserving the Divine Empyrean methods in extended form."},{title:"Hǎiqióng Bái zhēnrén yǔlù 海瓊白真人語錄",note:"The recorded sayings of Bai Yuchan, set down by his disciples — the clearest single statement of the identity of inner alchemy and thunder ritual, from the man in whom the two met."},{title:"Judith M. Boltz, A Survey of Taoist Literature: Tenth to Seventeenth Centuries",note:"Institute of East Asian Studies, University of California, Berkeley, 1987. The essential map of Song and Yuan Daoist texts, and still the first place to check an attribution."},{title:"Florian C. Reiter, Basic Conditions of Taoist Thunder Magic",note:"Harrassowitz, 2007. A close study of the thunder corpus, its rubrics and its theology."},{title:"Mark R. E. Meulenbeld, Demonic Warfare: Daoism, Territorial Networks, and the History of a Ming Novel",note:"University of Hawaii Press, 2015. On thunder ritual, exorcism and the marshals as they moved between liturgy, local cult and fiction."}]},{slug:"meditation-and-visualisation",title:"Meditation and Visualisation",native:"存思",translit:"cúnsī",summary:"Not imagination but actualisation. Cúnsī makes present what it describes — and the corpus that perfected it was itself received in vision, by a visionary writing at night in a Jiangnan household between 364 and 370.",paragraphs:["The verb governs the discipline. Cún 存 means to make present, to preserve in being, to cause to subsist — it is what one does to a thing that would otherwise not be there — and cúnsī 存思, with its variant cúnxiǎng 存想, therefore does not mean 'to imagine' in the modern sense of entertaining a fiction. To visualise a god in this tradition is to bring the god into presence at a location, ordinarily inside one's own body, and to hold him there. The practice is accordingly dense with specification: the texts give names, titles, dimensions, complexions, robes and insignia, and they give them because an approximate visualisation is understood to fail in the way an approximate address fails. This is what makes the corpus so strange to read. Pages that look like inventories of court dress are technical instructions, and a reader without the accompanying oral transmission has been handed the inventory and not the technique.","The great visionary corpus is Shàngqīng 上清 (Shang-ch'ing), Highest Clarity, and it has an unusually precise origin. Between 364 and 370, in the household of the Xu family at Jurong near modern Nanjing, a visionary in their service named Yáng Xī 楊羲 (330–386) received nocturnal visitations from Perfected beings — chief among them Lady Wèi Huácún 魏華存 (251–334), who had died a generation earlier — and wrote down what they dictated: poems, biographies of the transcendents, descriptions of the heavens, instructions for meditation, and a good deal of intelligence about the celestial hierarchy. He wrote for two patrons, Xǔ Mì 許謐 and his son Xǔ Huì 許翽. The manuscripts afterwards dispersed, were forged and traded, and were then hunted down, authenticated by handwriting, annotated and edited by Táo Hóngjǐng 陶弘景 (456–536) into the Zhēngào 真誥, 'Declarations of the Perfected', finished about 499. Tao's editorial apparatus is one of the notable achievements of premodern philology anywhere: he distinguishes the hands of Yang and the two Xus, tracks individual sheets through successive owners, and names the forger Wáng Língqī 王靈期, who fabricated Shangqing scriptures for profit and whose products Tao patiently separates out. A tradition that preserves at its foundation its own editor's account of which of its revelations are spurious is not a naive tradition.","The body these texts work upon is inhabited. It has palaces and chambers, offices and residents: gods of the five viscera with names and colours, a company of Perfected who may be summoned to their stations, and three principal fields — the níwán 泥丸, the 'muddy pellet' in the head, whose odd name may or may not be a transcription of nirvāṇa and has been argued both ways; the jiànggōng 絳宮, the crimson palace at the heart; and the lower field below the navel. The Huángtíng jīng 黃庭經, 'Scripture of the Yellow Court', is the classic text of this anatomy, anonymous, surviving in an outer and an inner version whose dates are unsettled — the outer probably Han or Wei, the inner associated with the Shangqing milieu — and written in seven-syllable verse meant to be chanted while the gods it names are actualised. The best-known picture of the interior landscape, the Nèijīng tú 內經圖, the title also written 內景圖, which renders the torso as a mountain valley with waterwheels, ploughmen, a weaving girl and a pagoda for a spine, should be enjoyed with its date attached: it is a stele of the late nineteenth century at the Baiyun Guan in Beijing, a brilliant late synthesis and not an ancient chart.","Two older practices run underneath. Guarding the One, shǒuyī 守一, is attested well before Shangqing — in the Taiping jing, and in Ge Hong, who describes the One as having a name, a colour and a measurable height and as residing by turns in the three fields, so that even this most abstract-sounding discipline turns out to be concretely visual; Shangqing elaborates it into the guarding of the Three Ones. And the body is not populated only by allies. It carries the three corpses, sānshī 三尸, also called the three worms: parasitic residents of the three fields who profit by their host's death and therefore work against him. On each gēngshēn 庚申 night, the fifty-seventh of the sexagenary cycle, they leave the sleeping body and ascend to report his misdeeds to the celestial registrars, who dock his allotted lifespan accordingly. The countermeasure was to stay awake, and the gengshen vigil became a social institution — parties, in effect, held every sixty days — which travelled to Japan as kōshin and left a substantial material record there in stone markers. The corpses are also the reason for the avoidance of grain, since grain is what they feed on, which is taken up under Immortality Practices.","Against all this stands a subtractive tradition. Zuòwàng 坐忘, 'sitting in forgetfulness', is a phrase from the Zhuāngzǐ 莊子 (Chuang-tzu), where Yan Hui reports to a startled Confucius that he has let limbs and trunk fall away, dismissed hearing and sight, and merged with the great thoroughfare. Sīmǎ Chéngzhēn 司馬承禎 (647–735), twelfth patriarch of Shangqing and an intimate of the Tang court, is the author to whom the Zuòwàng lùn 坐忘論 is ascribed; it arranges the ascent in seven stages from reverent trust, through the severing of involvements, the collecting of the mind, the simplification of affairs, true observation and profound stability, to the attainment of the Dao. It should be noted that a stele version of the text differs from the canonical one and that the authorship of parts is disputed. The difference from cunsi is real, and it is the axis on which much of the later history turns. Visualisation builds, specifies and populates; zuowang, and the quiet-sitting, jìngzuò 靜坐, that descends from it, strip away — and the Quanzhen emphasis on clarity and stillness, qīngjìng 清靜, belongs on that side. But the succession is not clean, and it is a mistake to draw it as one. Neidan retains visualisation inside its refining sequence, and cunsi never left the liturgy at all: a priest today, before he dispatches a petition, still actualises the officers who are to carry it, exactly as the medieval manuals instruct him to."],terms:[{native:"存思",translit:"cúnsī",gloss:"Visualisation; more exactly actualisation — cún 存 means to make present, not to imagine"},{native:"上清",translit:"Shàngqīng",gloss:"Highest Clarity; the visionary tradition revealed to Yang Xi between 364 and 370, and the summit of the ordination ladder"},{native:"真人",translit:"zhēnrén",gloss:"Perfected being; the class of transcendents who dictated the Shangqing revelations, ranked above ordinary immortals"},{native:"守一",translit:"shǒuyī",gloss:"'Guarding the One'; a meditation older than Shangqing, in which the One has a name, a colour and a height"},{native:"坐忘",translit:"zuòwàng",gloss:"'Sitting in forgetfulness'; a phrase from the Zhuangzi, made into a seven-stage discipline in the treatise ascribed to Sima Chengzhen"},{native:"三尸",translit:"sānshī",gloss:"The three corpses or worms; parasitic residents of the body who report its misdeeds to the celestial registrars"},{native:"泥丸",translit:"níwán",gloss:"The 'muddy pellet', the upper field in the head; whether the name transcribes nirvāṇa is disputed"},{native:"靜坐",translit:"jìngzuò",gloss:"Quiet sitting; the later subtractive meditation, to be distinguished from the image-rich visualisation it partly displaced"}],texts:[{title:"Tao Hongjing, Zhēngào 真誥",note:"Declarations of the Perfected, completed about 499: Yang Xi's revelations recovered, authenticated and annotated, with Tao's own account of which manuscripts are forgeries."},{title:"Huángtíng nèijǐng jīng 黃庭內景經",note:"Scripture of the Yellow Court, inner version; anonymous, of unsettled date — the classic map of the body's resident gods, in chantable seven-syllable verse."},{title:"Shàngqīng dàdòng zhēnjīng 上清大洞真經",note:"The supreme scripture of the Shangqing corpus, revealed rather than authored on its own account, whose recitation and accompanying visualisations stood at the top of the medieval ordination sequence."},{title:"Zuòwàng lùn 坐忘論, ascribed to Sima Chengzhen",note:"The seven stages of sitting in forgetfulness; the ascription is traditional and parts of the text are disputed. Translated and studied by Livia Kohn as Seven Steps to the Tao (Steyler, 1987), which also treats the divergent stele version."},{title:"Isabelle Robinet, Taoist Meditation: The Mao-shan Tradition of Great Purity",note:"SUNY Press, 1993 (French original 1979). The standard study of Shangqing visualisation, its texts and its methods."},{title:"Stephen Bokenkamp, Early Daoist Scriptures",note:"University of California Press, 1997. Includes annotated translation of Shangqing material and a careful account of the revelation and its household setting."}]},{slug:"chinese-astrology",title:"Chinese Astrology",native:"星命",translit:"xīngmìng",summary:"The sky is an administration, and it keeps files. The Dipper holds the ledger of lifespans, the lunar lodges divide the year and the sexagenary cycle counts the days — while the horoscopic systems most people now mean by Chinese astrology are recent arrivals wearing old pedigrees.",paragraphs:["The Northern Dipper, Běidǒu 北斗 (Pei-tou), is the centre of the Chinese religious sky. Seven visible stars, with two invisible attendants making the Nine Emperors in the Daoist reckoning, it turns about the pole through the year and so serves as the clock of the heavens; and because it turns, and because what turns governs, it is the seat of destiny. A proverbial formula preserved in the fourth-century tale collection Sōushén jì 搜神記 has the Northern Dipper govern death and the Southern govern life, and the Dipper accordingly holds the ledger of allotted lifespans. Each person has a natal destiny, běnmìng 本命, tied to the sexagenary designation of the year of birth, and a natal star among the Dipper's seven; the Scripture of the Northern Dipper and Original Destiny exists precisely in order to petition it for an extension, and is recited still. On the altar the priest walks the Dipper — bùgāng tàdǒu 步罡踏斗, 'pacing the guideline and treading the Dipper' — tracing its pattern with his feet upon a diagram, so that his steps carry him through the stars. The gait is the Pace of Yu, yǔbù 禹步, a limping step attributed to the flood-quelling sage-king, and it is genuinely ancient: versions appear in excavated Qin and Han daybooks, well before there was a Daoist church to inherit them.","The zodiacal frame is the twenty-eight lunar lodges, èrshíbā xiù 二十八宿: unequal segments of the celestial equator marked by determinative stars and grouped into four palaces of seven under the Azure Dragon of the east, the Vermilion Bird of the south, the White Tiger of the west and the Dark Warrior of the north. Their antiquity is a matter of archaeology rather than tradition — the complete list of twenty-eight appears written on a lacquer chest from the tomb of Marquis Yi of Zeng, sealed in 433 BCE. The counting apparatus is the ten Heavenly Stems, tiāngān 天干, and the twelve Earthly Branches, dìzhī 地支, combined in fixed pairs to yield the cycle of sixty, liùshí jiǎzǐ 六十甲子. Here too the dating is firm and worth stating precisely, because it is so often blurred: the sixty-cycle is used to number days in Shang oracle-bone inscriptions of the thirteenth century BCE and has run without interruption since, which makes it the longest continuously operating count of days known anywhere. Its application to years is a later development, standard from the Han. A system used to name days for a millennium before it was used to name years is a system whose year-astrology cannot be as old as its adherents sometimes suggest.","Two horoscopic systems dominate present-day practice, and both wear pedigrees longer than their texts. Zǐwēi dǒushù 紫微斗數, 'Purple Tenuity Dipper Numbers', distributes a set of largely notional stars — Purple Tenuity is the polar enclosure — across twelve palaces derived from the birth data, and is traditionally ascribed to Chén Tuán 陳摶 (d. 989), the Song recluse to whom a great many things are ascribed. No Song text of the system survives. The earliest substantial ones are Ming, the Zǐwēi dǒushù quánshū 紫微斗數全書 being a compilation of that period, and the honest statement is that the attribution to Chen Tuan is traditional and undemonstrated while the system as practised is a Ming and later construction. The Four Pillars, sìzhù 四柱, better known as the Eight Characters, bāzì 八字, reads a life from the stem-and-branch pairs of the year, month, day and hour of birth. Its traditional ancestor is Lǐ Xūzhōng 李虛中 (761–813), whose epitaph Han Yu wrote, and who is credited with reckoning fate from year, month and day — three pillars, not four. The decisive turn, making the day stem the pivot of the reading and adding the hour, is attributed to Xú Zǐpíng 徐子平, traditionally placed in the tenth century and himself poorly documented, which is why the method is called Ziping; but the standard texts, the anonymous Yuānhǎi zǐpíng 淵海子平 and Wàn Mínyīng's 萬民英 Sānmìng tōnghuì 三命通會 of 1578, are Ming. This is late material working an ancient toolkit, and saying so takes nothing from it.","What holds the sky together is not influence but administration. The stars are offices — xīngguān 星官 means 'stellar officials', and Sima Qian's treatise on them in the Records of the Grand Historian is called the 'Book of the Celestial Offices' — and their business is record-keeping. The Director of Destinies, Sīmìng 司命, appears as a deity already in the Warring States hymns of the Chuci and is later domesticated into the Stove God of every kitchen, who ascends annually to report on the household's conduct. The Officers of Heaven, Earth and Water keep the ledgers to which the Celestial Masters addressed their written confessions. The three corpses report on gengshen nights. The Dipper docks days. By the Ming the logic had been made explicit and arithmetical in the ledgers of merit and demerit, gōngguògé 功過格, in which a person scored his own conduct daily against a published tariff and kept the accounts himself. The premise is consistent across two thousand years: conduct is recorded, the record is kept elsewhere, and lifespan is the currency in which the balance is settled. Which is why so much of Chinese esoteric practice is concerned less with learning the future than with amending the file."],terms:[{native:"北斗",translit:"Běidǒu",gloss:"The Northern Dipper; clock of the heavens, seat of destiny, keeper of the ledger of lifespans. Simplified merges 斗 with 鬥 'to fight'"},{native:"二十八宿",translit:"èrshíbā xiù",gloss:"The twenty-eight lunar lodges; unequal segments of the celestial equator, attested complete by 433 BCE"},{native:"天干",translit:"tiāngān",gloss:"The ten Heavenly Stems; the first of the two counting series"},{native:"地支",translit:"dìzhī",gloss:"The twelve Earthly Branches; paired with the stems, and separately correlated with the twelve animals"},{native:"六十甲子",translit:"liùshí jiǎzǐ",gloss:"The sexagenary cycle; used for days since the Shang oracle bones, and for years only from the Han"},{native:"紫微斗數",translit:"Zǐwēi dǒushù",gloss:"'Purple Tenuity Dipper Numbers'; the star-chart system ascribed to Chen Tuan but attested only from the Ming"},{native:"八字",translit:"bāzì",gloss:"The Eight Characters, also the Four Pillars 四柱; the stem-and-branch pairs of year, month, day and hour of birth"},{native:"本命",translit:"běnmìng",gloss:"Natal destiny; the sexagenary designation of one's birth and the Dipper star that governs it"}],texts:[{title:"Sima Qian, Shǐjì 史記, the 'Tiānguān shū' 天官書",note:"The Book of the Celestial Offices, about 100 BCE — the foundational description of the sky as an administration, office by office."},{title:"Tàishàng xuánlíng běidǒu běnmìng yánshēng zhēnjīng 太上玄靈北斗本命延生真經",note:"The Scripture of the Northern Dipper, for the extension of the allotted lifespan; anonymous and revealed on its own account, and among the most widely recited Daoist texts, then and now."},{title:"Qútán Xīdá 瞿曇悉達, Kāiyuán zhànjīng 開元占經",note:"Compiled 718–726 by Gautama Siddha, an astronomer of Indian descent at the Tang court; the great encyclopaedia of omen astrology, and a channel for Indian material."},{title:"Zǐwēi dǒushù quánshū 紫微斗數全書",note:"The compendium of Purple Star astrology, ascribed to Chen Tuan but a Ming compilation; the basis of the system as practised today."},{title:"Wan Minying 萬民英, Sānmìng tōnghuì 三命通會",note:"1578. The great Ming synthesis of Four Pillars fate-calculation, and still the standard reference within the tradition."},{title:"Ho Peng Yoke, Chinese Mathematical Astrology: Reaching Out to the Stars",note:"RoutledgeCurzon, 2003. The clearest English account of the technical systems, with their mechanics set out and their dates handled honestly."}]},{slug:"yijing-cosmology",title:"Yijing Cosmology",native:"周易",translit:"Zhōu Yì",summary:"A Bronze Age divination manual with a philosophical library grown over it — and, from the Han onward, a supply of structures so accommodating that the alchemists used its sixty-four hexagrams as a clock for the fire.",paragraphs:["Strip the commentaries away and what remains is a manual for consulting an oracle. The Zhōu Yì 周易, the 'Changes of Zhou' — the Yìjīng 易經 (I Ching in the older spelling) — consists of sixty-four figures, each with a short statement, guàcí 卦辭, and six line statements, yáocí 爻辭, whose language is terse, image-laden, frequently obscure, and punctuated by a small vocabulary of verdicts: jí 吉 auspicious, xiōng 凶 inauspicious, huǐ 悔 regret, lìn 吝 distress, wú jiù 無咎 no blame. Current scholarship places this core in the Western Zhou, around the ninth century BCE, and reads much of its imagery as belonging to the world of Bronze Age omen-taking — sacrificial animals, captives, military expeditions, the flight of birds — rather than to philosophy; Richard Rutt subtitled his translation 'A Bronze Age Document' for exactly this reason. Whatever else the book became, it began as an instrument for putting questions to something that answered in figures.","The philosophy is an overlay, and the overlay has a name. The Ten Wings, Shíyì 十翼, are the appended treatises that made the manual a classic: the Judgements and the Images in two parts each, the Appended Statements or Great Treatise, Xìcí 繫辭, again in two parts, the Words of the Text, and the Discussion, Sequence and Miscellany of the trigrams. Tradition ascribes them to Confucius. The doubt is not modern — Ōuyáng Xiū 歐陽修 (1007–1072) argued against Confucian authorship in the eleventh century — and the settled scholarly view now places them in the Warring States and early Han, by several hands over some centuries. The decisive evidence for how open the tradition once was came out of the ground in 1973: the silk manuscript of the Yi from Mawangdui, buried in 168 BCE, carries the sixty-four hexagrams in an entirely different order from the received text, together with commentaries not in the canon at all. With the Warring States bamboo Yi in the Shanghai Museum and the Han bamboo manuscript from Fuyang, it establishes that the received arrangement is one arrangement among several that circulated, and that the book we read is the survivor of a competition rather than a monolith.","The figures are built from broken and unbroken lines. Three lines give the eight trigrams, bāguà 八卦 — Qián ☰ heaven, Duì ☱ lake, Lí ☲ fire, Zhèn ☳ thunder, Xùn ☴ wind, Kǎn ☵ water, Gèn ☶ mountain, Kūn ☷ earth — and two trigrams stacked give the sixty-four hexagrams. The traditional account of the authorship is layered rather than single, and the layers should be kept apart. The Han bibliographic treatise gives the classic formula of three sages across three ages: Fú Xī 伏羲 devised the trigrams, King Wen, Wén Wáng 文王, doubled them and supplied the statements during his imprisonment, and Confucius wrote the Wings. The further refinement that assigns the line statements to King Wen's son, the Duke of Zhou, is not in that treatise; it belongs to the Han commentator Ma Rong and was standardised by the Tang scholiast Kong Yingda. All of it is tradition, and is offered here as tradition. The two circular arrangements of the trigrams require the same care. The 'posterior to heaven' order, hòutiān 後天, associated with King Wen, derives from the Discussion of the Trigrams and is genuinely early. The 'prior to heaven' order, xiāntiān 先天, credited to Fu Xi and beloved of every later diagram, is a Song construction: it belongs to Shào Yōng 邵雍 (1011–1077) and the transmission he claimed from Chen Tuan, and it was presented as immemorial by men who had lately made it. Since the xiantian arrangement is precisely the one on which alchemical writing depends, this matters a good deal.","Two consultation methods are described. The Great Treatise gives a compressed account of a procedure using fifty yarrow stalks, of which one is set aside and the remaining forty-nine are repeatedly divided and counted off in fours, three operations yielding one line and eighteen a whole hexagram, the resulting numbers six, seven, eight and nine distinguishing old and young yin and yang. The account is so compressed that the standard reconstruction owes as much to Zhū Xī 朱熹, who appended a rubric for divination to his Zhōuyì běnyì of 1177, as it does to the Han. The three-coin method, in which coins are thrown six times, is far quicker and is traditionally traced to the Huǒzhūlín 火珠林, a Song manual of coin divination of uncertain authorship. It is worth being precise about how the two differ, since the point is often garbled: both produce moving lines at the same overall rate of one line in four, but the yarrow procedure makes a changing yang three times as likely as a changing yin, in the proportion three in sixteen against one in sixteen, while the coins make the two equally likely at one in eight each — which is either a corruption or a legitimate variant, depending on whom one reads. What both methods produce is not a static verdict. Lines marked as old are moving lines; they turn into their opposites and yield a second hexagram, so that a consultation delivers a transformation, zhīguà 之卦 — this figure going to that one, in the very idiom the Zuǒ zhuàn 左傳 uses for the twenty-odd divinations it records from the Spring and Autumn courts. A hexagram is a situation in motion, read as a passage from one configuration to another, and that is the single most important thing to understand about the book.","Because the sixty-four figures form a complete and ordered set that changes by rule, they made an irresistible calendar. Han cosmology had already correlated hexagrams with the divisions of the year in the guàqì 卦氣 system of Mèng Xǐ 孟喜 and Jīng Fáng 京房, and had matched the trigrams to the Heavenly Stems in the nàjiǎ 納甲 scheme so that the phases of the moon could be read off the figures. Alchemical writing took the whole apparatus over as its clock. In the Zhōuyì cāntóng qì 周易參同契 — attributed to Wèi Bóyáng 魏伯陽 and traditionally placed in the second century, though the received text is composite and in part later, and the attribution is not independently attested — Qian and Kun are the crucible and the furnace, Kan and Li are the working agents, and the remaining sixty hexagrams are distributed across the days of the cycle to govern the huǒhòu 火候, the 'fire times': when heat is raised, when withdrawn, when the operation rests. The twelve sovereign hexagrams, whose yang lines advance and retreat in sequence, map the waxing and waning of the year and of the work within it. Whether the Cantong qi was originally about minerals, about the body, or about neither and was always a cosmological poem, is disputed and probably unanswerable. What is certain is that from the Tang onward it was read as the ancestor of both alchemies, and that the Yijing's structures reached most later practitioners already dissolved in it."],terms:[{native:"周易",translit:"Zhōu Yì",gloss:"The Changes of Zhou; the divinatory core, distinguished from the Yìjīng 易經 as core plus commentaries — I Ching in Wade-Giles"},{native:"卦",translit:"guà",gloss:"A trigram or hexagram; the figure itself, as against its statements"},{native:"爻",translit:"yáo",gloss:"A line of a figure, broken or unbroken; the unit that moves, and so the unit that makes a reading a process"},{native:"八卦",translit:"bāguà",gloss:"The eight trigrams; three-line figures correlated with heaven, lake, fire, thunder, wind, water, mountain and earth"},{native:"十翼",translit:"Shíyì",gloss:"The Ten Wings; the appended treatises, of several hands and some centuries, that turned a divination manual into a philosophical classic"},{native:"繫辭",translit:"Xìcí",gloss:"The Appended Statements or Great Treatise; source of the cosmology and of the account of the yarrow procedure"},{native:"先天",translit:"xiāntiān",gloss:"'Prior to heaven'; the trigram arrangement credited to Fu Xi but in fact a Song construction of Shao Yong's circle"},{native:"火候",translit:"huǒhòu",gloss:"The 'fire times'; the firing schedule of alchemy, mapped onto the hexagram cycle — the hinge between the Changes and both alchemies"}],texts:[{title:"Zhōu Yì 周易 with the Ten Wings",note:"The received text: a Western Zhou divinatory core with Warring States and Han philosophical appendices, anonymous throughout, transmitted together since the Han as a single classic."},{title:"The Mawangdui silk manuscript of the Yi",note:"Buried 168 BCE, recovered 1973: a different hexagram order and commentaries outside the canon. Translated and studied by Edward Shaughnessy, I Ching: The Classic of Changes (Ballantine, 1996)."},{title:"Wang Bi 王弼, Zhōuyì zhù 周易注",note:"The commentary of Wang Bi (226–249), which read the book philosophically and displaced the Han image-and-number schools for a thousand years."},{title:"Zhu Xi, Zhōuyì běnyì 周易本義",note:"1177. The Original Meaning of the Changes, which restored the book's divinatory character to respectability and supplied the rubric on which modern yarrow practice rests."},{title:"Zhōuyì cāntóng qì 周易參同契",note:"The Token for Joining the Three: the text that fused the Changes with alchemy and gave both alchemies their firing schedule; attributed to Wei Boyang, but composite and in part later. Pregadio, The Seal of the Unity of the Three (2011)."},{title:"Richard Rutt, The Book of Changes (Zhouyi): A Bronze Age Document",note:"Curzon, 1996. A translation that deliberately strips away the Wings to show what the manual said before it was a classic."}]},{slug:"immortality-practices",title:"Immortality Practices",native:"仙",translit:"xiān",summary:"Transcendence was held to be a craft. Ge Hong insisted it is achieved by technique and not conferred by grace — though he insisted equally that no technique works for a man without accumulated merit, which complicates the slogan considerably.",paragraphs:["The word first. Xiān 仙 is written with the elements for person and mountain; its older form, xiān 僊, is built instead on a phonetic meaning to rise or to remove, and the Han dictionaries gloss it as living long and departing in transformation, or simply as one who grows old and does not die. English has no good equivalent. 'Immortal' overstates, since many xian (hsien) are described as long-lived rather than eternal and some are demoted or die; 'transcendent', now the preferred rendering among sinologists and used here alongside it, understates the physicality of what is meant, since the transformation happens to a body. The grades are enumerated differently in different sources, and the schemes do not reconcile. Ge Hong, citing a scripture of the transcendents, gives three: celestial transcendents, tiānxiān 天仙, who rise bodily into the heavens; earthbound transcendents, dìxiān 地仙, who take up residence in the famous mountains; and those released by the corpse, shījiěxiān 尸解仙, who depart by the method described below and whom he ranks lowest of the three. The later ZhongLü literature gives five, inserting ghost- and human-transcendents at the bottom. That two authoritative sources count differently is normal in this field and should not be smoothed away.","Shījiě 尸解, 'release by means of the corpse', is the most peculiar and the most revealing of the modes. The adept appears to die and is buried; the body in the coffin is a substitute — a sword, a staff, a sandal, a length of bamboo — and the adept has gone elsewhere. Sub-varieties are distinguished by the object left behind or the element passed through: release by sword, jiànjiě 劍解, by staff, by fire, by water. The Shangqing sources, which discuss the technique at length, treat it as a real but inferior departure, a back door for those who could not manage the front. Its usefulness to the tradition becomes obvious once the alchemical chapters have been read, since it supplies an interpretation for the adept who takes an elixir and dies; some texts go further and speak of elixirs of shijie, preparations whose intended effect is the apparent death itself. A reader may take that as evasion, and in part it is. It is also a community doing what communities do with their dead, which is to give the death a meaning the survivors can live inside. Both readings should be held at once.","The regimens are older than the church, and are attested by excavation rather than by legend. Grain avoidance, bìgǔ 辟穀 — abstention specifically from the five grains, on the reasoning that grain produces the coarse sediment of the mortal body and feeds the three corpses that want the adept dead — appears in a manuscript from Mawangdui, buried in 168 BCE, which pairs it with the ingestion of breath; the same tomb yielded an illustrated chart of gymnastic exercises, dǎoyǐn 導引. Breath practices are named across the corpus: fúqì 服氣, ingesting the breaths, xíngqì 行氣, circulating them, and the ideal called tāixī 胎息, embryonic breathing, in which the adept is said to breathe as a foetus does, without nose or mouth. The oldest Chinese text on the circulation of breath is not a book at all but an inscription of some forty-five characters, the Xíngqì yùpèi míng 行氣玉佩銘, cut into a twelve-sided jade knob of the late Warring States. This magazine names these practices and gives no regimen for any of them; and one plain observation is owed alongside the description. Prolonged grain avoidance is prolonged undernutrition, the substitute preparations included minerals of the kind discussed under External Alchemy, and the sources themselves record adepts who weakened, sickened and died. The tradition was not naive about this. Neither should its readers be.","The geography of transcendence is concrete. Xīwángmǔ 西王母, the Queen Mother of the West, appears in the Classic of Mountains and Seas, Shānhǎi jīng 山海經, as a fierce being with tiger's teeth and a leopard's tail, and is transformed by the Han into the great goddess of immortality — powerful enough that in 3 BCE a spontaneous mass movement in her name swept across the empire, crowds passing tokens from hand to hand, an episode the Han history records with alarm. Everyone knows the orchard of peaches that ripen once in three thousand years, and it should be dated honestly: the episode belongs to the Hàn Wǔdì nèizhuàn 漢武帝內傳, an anonymous romance of the Shangqing milieu about Emperor Wu of the Han, variously dated between the fourth and the sixth century and falsely ascribed in the tradition to Ban Gu. It is not in the early sources; the name pántáo 蟠桃 by which the peaches are now universally known, and the great banquet that goes with them, belong to a still later elaboration and above all to Ming fiction. Nearer to hand are the grotto-heavens and blessed lands, dòngtiān fúdì 洞天福地 — ten great grotto-heavens, thirty-six lesser and seventy-two blessed lands, catalogued by Sima Chengzhen and then canonically by Du Guangting in 901. A grotto-heaven is a complete world inside a mountain, with its own light, its own seasons and its own administration, entered through a cave mouth by those who know the way; the earthbound transcendents live there. The list is not mythological geography. It names actual mountains, which can be visited, and most of them carry temples still.","Ge Hong's argument, made against sceptical contemporaries who thought the whole business a fraud, is that transcendence is a shù 術: a technique, an art, something learnable, transmissible and effective, standing to ordinary mortality as medicine stands to illness. Immortality is not conferred by a god upon a favourite; it is achieved, by the right procedures, in the right order, with the right transmission. That is a genuinely distinctive position, and it is the one on which any comparison with grace-centred traditions should be hung. But Ge Hong immediately qualifies it, and the qualification is not decorative: he holds that no elixir will work for a man who has not accumulated merit, that a would-be transcendent must perform a specified number of good deeds and that a single evil act resets the count, and that a master will not transmit to the unworthy. Robert Campany has argued that the enterprise is better read as a set of social relations — who transmits to whom, on what terms, under what obligations — than as a private technology. So the honest formulation is a compound one: transcendence is earned rather than given, and the earning comprises both a technique and a life. Nor is this a closed chapter. Lü Dongbin and the eight transcendents have busy temples today, dietetic and breathing disciplines continue within Daoist orders under supervision, and the mountains on Du Guangting's list receive pilgrims who are not being metaphorical."],terms:[{native:"仙",translit:"xiān",gloss:"Transcendent, immortal; written person-plus-mountain, older form 僊. Wade-Giles hsien"},{native:"天仙",translit:"tiānxiān",gloss:"Celestial transcendent; the highest of Ge Hong's three grades, who rises bodily into the heavens"},{native:"地仙",translit:"dìxiān",gloss:"Earthbound transcendent; who does not ascend but takes up residence in the famous mountains and the grotto-heavens"},{native:"尸解",translit:"shījiě",gloss:"Release by means of the corpse; a substitute is left in the coffin. Traditional orthography keeps 尸 here, distinct from 屍 'corpse'; simplified merges the two"},{native:"辟穀",translit:"bìgǔ",gloss:"Avoidance of grain; simplified 辟谷, where 穀 'grain' collapses into 谷 'valley' and the sense is lost"},{native:"服氣",translit:"fúqì",gloss:"'Ingesting the breaths'; the dietetic-respiratory practice paired with grain avoidance from the Mawangdui manuscripts onward"},{native:"胎息",translit:"tāixī",gloss:"Embryonic breathing; the ideal of breathing as a foetus does, without nose or mouth"},{native:"洞天",translit:"dòngtiān",gloss:"Grotto-heaven; a complete world within a mountain, with its own light and administration, paired with the blessed lands 福地"}],texts:[{title:"Ge Hong, Bàopǔzǐ nèipiān 抱朴子內篇",note:"The defence of transcendence as technique, the three grades of xian, and the insistence that merit is a precondition of any elixir working at all."},{title:"Ge Hong, Shénxiān zhuàn 神仙傳",note:"Traditions of Divine Transcendents; the received text is itself much reconstituted from later encyclopaedias. Translated and studied by Robert Ford Campany, To Live as Long as Heaven and Earth (University of California Press, 2002)."},{title:"Lièxiān zhuàn 列仙傳",note:"Traditions of Exemplary Transcendents, traditionally ascribed to Liu Xiang but in fact pseudonymous and probably of the second century CE — the earliest collection of its kind."},{title:"Quègǔ shíqì 卻穀食氣",note:"Eliminating Grain and Ingesting Breath: an anonymous manuscript from Mawangdui, buried 168 BCE, and the earliest attestation of the dietetic and respiratory regimen."},{title:"Du Guangting, Dòngtiān fúdì yuèdú míngshān jì 洞天福地嶽瀆名山記",note:"901. The canonical catalogue of the ten grotto-heavens, the thirty-six lesser and the seventy-two blessed lands, following Sima Chengzhen's earlier list."},{title:"Robert Ford Campany, Making Transcendents: Ascetics and Social Memory in Early Medieval China",note:"University of Hawaii Press, 2009. Reads the quest for transcendence as a social practice of transmission, reputation and memory rather than a private technology."}]}]};export{e as default};
